# A pH map of the kitchen — and what a pH meter cannot always say
#
# Five things from a kitchen, one meter. Four of them answer. The fifth is
# the interesting one, and it is not the bench being lazy.
register lv2

add v1 Haushaltsessig_5% 50mL
measure v1 ph

new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 NaHCO3 5g
measure v2 ph

new beaker
add v3 water 100mL
add v3 Na2CO3 5g
measure v3 ph

# Lemon juice. Sour, and the sourness is citric acid — which the loaded
# database defines a citrate for, so the meter can price it.
new beaker
add v4 Zitronensaft 50mL
measure v4 ph

# Apple juice. Also sour, also a fruit acid, also resolved as itself — and
# the meter cannot price it. Malic acid is in the registry and in the
# glass; a malate is in no shipped database, so its two protons are not in
# anybody's speciation. The bench says exactly that instead of returning a
# number that would look like the others.
new beaker
add v5 apple_juice 50mL
measure v5 ph

# That is the whole lesson. The difference between v4 and v5 is not the
# chemistry, the fruit, or how carefully the recipe was written. It is
# whether somebody, somewhere, has published a thermodynamic model for that
# particular anion — and when they have not, the honest answer is a
# sentence rather than a number.
